From: LuneOS <webos-ports@googlegroups.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] app_runtime: exit the shell when the compositor closes the window

ShellWindow::WindowHostClose() was an empty stub, so a close request from the
compositor -- closing the card in the webOS card shell -- was received and
dropped.

The path up to that point is already complete: the webOS shell surface delivers
the close through WaylandWindowWebos::HandleWindowHostClose() and
DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::OnWindowHostClose() to the NativeEventDelegate,
which is ShellWindow. The path after it is complete too: WindowClosing() notifies
the ShellWindowDelegate and observers, and Shell::OnWindowClosing() recognises
the main window and calls Shell::Shutdown(). Only the stub in the middle was
missing, which left Shutdown() reachable only from a page calling window.close().

For a browsershell app the effect is that closing the card destroys the window
but leaves the whole browser_shell process running -- renderers, GPU process and
all -- with nothing on screen. Worse, SAM still considers the app running, so
relaunching re-shows the existing process instead of starting a new one: the app
appears to ignore a restart, and picks up neither new command-line flags nor a
newly installed webruntime.

Widget::Close() is used rather than ShellWindow::CloseNow(), because this runs
inside the platform window's own event dispatch and CloseNow() would
synchronously destroy the DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform and WaylandWindowWebos
still on the stack below. Close() defers the teardown, which is what
DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::OnCloseRequest() already does for an ordinary
close request, and it still ends in WindowClosing().

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/neva/app_runtime/app/app_runtime_shell_window.cc
+++ b/src/neva/app_runtime/app/app_runtime_shell_window.cc
@@ -141,7 +141,26 @@
 
 void ShellWindow::KeyboardLeave() {}
 
-void ShellWindow::WindowHostClose() {}
+// The compositor asking the window to go away -- closing the card in the webOS card shell, which
+// reaches us as a webOS shell-surface close through WaylandWindowWebos::HandleWindowHostClose() and
+// DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::OnWindowHostClose().
+//
+// Doing nothing here left the window gone but the process alive: WindowClosing() was never reached,
+// so neither the ShellWindowDelegate nor Shell::OnWindowClosing() heard about it, and
+// Shell::Shutdown() stayed reachable only from a page calling window.close(). For a browsershell app
+// that means closing the card leaves a whole browser_shell running -- renderers, GPU process and all
+// -- with nothing on screen; and since SAM still counts the app as running, relaunching re-shows the
+// old process rather than starting a new one.
+//
+// Widget::Close() rather than CloseNow(): this runs inside the platform window's own event dispatch,
+// and CloseNow() would synchronously destroy the DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform and the
+// WaylandWindowWebos still on the stack below us. Close() defers the teardown, which is exactly what
+// DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::OnCloseRequest() does with an ordinary close request. It still ends
+// in WindowClosing(), so the delegate and Shell::OnWindowClosing() run as they should.
+void ShellWindow::WindowHostClose() {
+  if (window_widget_)
+    window_widget_->Close();
+}
 
 void ShellWindow::WindowHostExposed() {}
 
